# Firmware build dependencies (CI jobs: "Device build", and the Release job that
# handles OTA_SIGNING_KEY). Compile with:
#   pip-compile --upgrade --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements/build.txt --strip-extras requirements/build.in
#
# `--upgrade` matters: without it pip-compile keeps every pin already in
# build.txt, so a bare `platformio` here silently re-locks to whatever version
# was there before. That is how a bump can look done and not be.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HISTORY, kept short. From 2026-07-26 to 2026-09-07 this file carried a
# KNOWN, ACCEPTED note for five starlette Dependabot alerts: platformio 6.1.19
# capped `starlette<0.53` while every patched starlette was >=1.0.1, so there
# was no upgrade path, and the vulnerable code (the `pio home` web UI) never
# loaded during `pio run`. platformio 6.2.0 widened that bound to `<2` (and
# uvicorn<1, click<8.6, marshmallow<5), so the lock now resolves to patched
# versions and the note is gone. If a future platformio release re-tightens a
# bound, hold platformio itself rather than pinning around it.
#
# The bump was checked the way a change to the toolchain that produces the
# signed OTA image has to be: crowpanel_obd built from the same source under
# 6.1.19 (twice) and 6.2.0, and the three firmware.bin compared byte for byte.
# This image is NOT reproducible as-is -- the core stamps __TIME__ into its
# "Software Info" block, and the app descriptor carries the ELF's SHA-256 and
# the image its own digest -- so two 6.1.19 builds already differ in exactly
# 67 bytes. The 6.2.0 build differs from either in the same 67 bytes and
# nowhere else; with those regions masked all three hash identically.
# tools/gen_sbom.py was run under both and produced the same 25 components.
# See the PR that introduced 6.2.0 for the offsets.
#
# pip-compile warns that `pip` (a declared dependency of platformio 6.2.0) is
# left unpinned as "unsafe". Expected: the venv's own pip satisfies it, and
# `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/build.txt` exits 0 with
# `pip check` clean.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
platformio
